[2025-04-30 13:40:24] PROPOSAL by USA:
Title: Untitled Proposal
Balanced Global Carbon Pricing Framework

1. Enact a Gradually Implemented Global Carbon Tax:  
   • Establish a worldwide carbon tax with phased implementation, beginning with major emitters.  
   • Recognize Common But Differentiated Responsibilities (CBDR-RC) by allowing lower initial rates for developing nations and providing them extended timelines to ramp up the tax rate.  

2. Commit Carbon Revenue to Technology & Capacity Building:  
   • Allocate a meaningful percentage of tax revenue toward global R&D in low-carbon and clean energy technologies.  
   • Provide targeted climate finance and technical assistance, enabling developing countries to transition without compromising their growth and poverty reduction objectives.  

3. Ensure Competitiveness and Just Transition:  
   • Provide transitional assistance (e.g., rebates, grants) to industries and communities most affected by decarbonization, minimizing economic disruptions.  
   • Include provisions to prevent carbon leakage, ensuring that industries are not incentivized to relocate to jurisdictions with weaker climate policies.  

This framework balances environmental urgency, respects varying national development priorities, and promotes stable economic growth through technological innovation. By coupling a phased carbon tax with robust finance and technology support, it ensures equitable responsibility sharing and minimal disruption to global competitiveness. All participating nations are invited to endorse these measures to foster a fair, effective, and sustainable approach to climate action.

[2025-04-30 13:40:31] PROPOSAL by China:
Title: Untitled Proposal
**Proposal Title: Equitable Measures for Climate Action: A Differentiated Approach to Global Carbon Tax**

**Preamble:**  
Recognizing the urgent need for robust measures to combat climate change, this proposal considers the varying historical responsibilities, economic capacities, and developmental priorities of nations under the principle of Common But Differentiated Responsibilities and Respective Capabilities (CBDR-RC). It seeks to ensure that global climate action is equitable, effective, and inclusive.

**Action Items:**  
1. **Differentiated Carbon Pricing Framework:**  
   - A global carbon tax, if implemented, must adopt a tiered system based on national circumstances, including per capita emissions, level of economic development, and historical contributions to global emissions.  
   - Developed nations should adopt the highest tax rates and provide financial contributions toward a global climate fund, which will support climate resilience and green development in developing nations.  
   - Developing economies should have lower or flexible tax rates to protect growth trajectories and avoid undue economic burdens. Least Developed Countries (LDCs) should be exempt.  

2. **Mandatory Climate Finance Contributions from Developed Nations:**  
   - Developed nations must commit to enhanced climate finance, meeting and exceeding the $100 billion annual pledge, with an emphasis on grants and concessional funding. These finances should prioritize renewable energy deployment, capacity-building, and technology transfer to help developing countries transition to low-carbon economies without compromising development.  

3. **Periodic Review Mechanism:**  
   - An inclusive global review mechanism should be established to assess the implementation and impact of the carbon tax and climate finance commitments every five years. This mechanism would allow adjustments to the framework based on emerging scientific evidence, technological advancements, and evolving economic conditions.  

**Conclusion:**  
This proposal balances environmental urgency with economic equity and inclusivity, ensuring that the burdens and benefits of climate action are fairly distributed. We urge all nations to adopt this framework in the spirit of shared but differentiated global responsibility.  

Thank you.

