Title: **Title:**
Equitable Global Carbon Tax Framework for Climate Justice and Sustainable Development

**Proposal:**  
Recognizing the urgent need for global cooperation to address climate change, and mindful of the principles of equity, Common But Differentiated Responsibilities and Respective Capabilities (CBDR-RC), and historical responsibility, this committee proposes the following framework for a Global Carbon Tax:

**Action Items:**  

1. **Differentiated Carbon Tax Structure:**  
   A tiered global carbon tax shall be implemented, whereby developed countries adopt a higher carbon price reflecting their historical emissions and greater financial capacity. Developing countries will be subject to a lower or phased-in rate, with least developed nations allowed exemptions until key developmental thresholds—such as basic energy access and poverty reduction—are met.

2. **Robust Climate Finance Mechanism:**  
   Revenue generated from the global carbon tax in developed countries will be earmarked for a dedicated Climate Justice Fund. This fund will support adaptation, mitigation, technology transfer, and capacity-building in developing and least developed countries, with transparent governance and periodic reviews to ensure effectiveness and accountability.

3. **Flexibility and National Circumstances:**  
   All participating nations may submit nationally determined plans outlining the pace and structure of their carbon tax implementation, subject to review but with respect for domestic priorities and socioeconomic conditions. Support will be provided for capacity-building in policy design and collection, to ensure fair and effective participation.

**This proposal aims to:**
- Create an ambitious, structured response to climate change;
- Reflect the diverse circumstances of all nations;
- Ensure mutual benefit and just transition without impeding sustainable development.

We submit this framework for committee debate and adoption.



